# The web image is built with `COPY . .` from ./web (see web/Dockerfile), so
# anything here that is not excluded ships in the production artifact.
#
# This matters most once a satellite adopts the help-media capture harness:
# `npm run help:capture` writes REAL signed session JWTs to e2e/.auth/*.json,
# and deploys run from the same devbox that runs the captures
# (`deploy-local <app> prod`), so those files are present at build time.
# Land this file BEFORE the capture harness, never after.
#
# Nothing below is needed by `next build` or by the running server.
e2e/
playwright.config.ts
playwright.help-capture.config.ts
e2e-results/
test-results/
playwright-report/

# Build scratch AND the host's dev modules. Excluding node_modules is
# load-bearing, not hygiene. The Dockerfile runs `npm ci` from the lockfile
# BEFORE `COPY . .`, and a host node_modules does not REPLACE that clean tree,
# it MERGES over it. So a build can pass on a package the lockfile does not
# carry, and `npm prune --omit=dev` at the end removes most of the evidence,
# which is why the wrong reasoning survived so long.
#
# Not theoretical. On 2026-09-06 a hubport prod build failed its type-check on
# `'mediaPlay' does not exist in type 'HelpViewStrings'` because the host clone
# carried microport-ui 0.45.1 while the lockfile pinned 0.46.0, and four host
# trees on that devbox were still skewed the same way afterwards. deploy-local
# builds from the live working tree, where web/node_modules is ~1GB, so the
# omission also costs that much build context per web build.
#
# All nine app repos exclude it as of 2026-09-06 (salesport 1a12993d,
# productport 560a645, EngagePort f4a8038 landed the last three). An earlier
# version of this comment named those three as omitting it deliberately; that
# was true when written and is now false. Amplification is why it matters here:
# a template shipping without this line hands the omission to every satellite
# minted from it.
node_modules
.next/

# Secrets a developer creates locally. Deploys run from the same devbox as
# local development, so a .env.local is present at build time on the machine
# that builds the image.
#
# The template ships no web/.env.local.example today (only SalesPort has one).
# If a satellite adds one, add `!.env.local.example` below so the layout inside
# the image still explains itself.
.env*
